Z||000000||saleenfiero||04-30-2001||01:30 AM||||I just checked out my e-brake and found it doesn't hold at all.  Now can someone tell me exactly where this adjusting bolt is under the car and whether this might actually fix the problem or not?  The handle inside the car ratches what seems like it should, but no hold.  Is there any point to using the e-brake like this for pad adjustment reasons or should I get it looked at by a pro?||||reg||6
Z||000001||FieroJoe||04-30-2001||07:48 AM||||The e-brake equalizer is pretty much in the vicinity of the left exhaust tip.  Look under it and you should see it hanging on a few hooks.  It has a threaded rod and a big nut on it for adjustment.  Turn the nut so it goes to the left and you will tighten up the slack in your cables.  Tighten them up enough so after about 4-5 clicks your car holds.<P>The nut is a fairly large size, like 15 or 18 mm or something around there.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: FieroJoe87<BR>87 Coupe 5spd<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||saleenfiero||04-30-2001||06:22 PM||||Hey, thanx for the 411.  I looked and I found the EQ, and now you say the nut has to go left to tighten?  It seems like there's alot of room to go on the bolt.  I'll go play with it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||saleenfiero||04-30-2001||06:59 PM||||Went out, tried tightening the thing, but the damn bolt won't tighten up any, just kinda of springs back to its original spot.  Does thing mean that is as far as it will go?  I just don't think I'm doing this right.||||reg||1
Z||000004||84Bill||05-01-2001||08:26 AM||||Ya need to hold the cable, its hexed at the end with a wrench or lock pliers then turn the nut!||||reg||3
